ies": {"value": "Images from Geograph Britain and Ireland|Ardcavan|Information field template with formatting|Images by David Hawgood", "source": "commons-categories", "hidden": ""}, "Assessments": {"value": "", "source": "commons-categories", "hidden": ""}, "GPSLatitude": {"value": "52.356730", "source": "commons-desc-page"}, "GPSLongitude": {"value": "-6.436300", "source": "commons-desc-page"}, "GPSMapDatum": {"value": "WGS-84", "source": "commons-desc-page", "hidden": ""}, "ImageDescription": {"value": "Flow control sluice on drain at Ardcavan. The drain runs all the way round the area of slobs which includes Wexford Wildfowl Reserve. It is intended to drain rainwater to the sea through the channel shown at <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rainwater_drain_exits_to_sea,_Ardcavan_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1237763.jpg\" title=\"File:Rainwater drain exits to sea, Ardcavan - geograph.org.uk - 1237763.jpg\">1237763</a>. It is arranged so that at low tide the sluice opens to allow rainwater to drain out, but at high tide it closes to stop sea water flooding in. When I saw it at high tide bthere was a slow flow of seawater into the drain. Staff in the Wildfowl Reserve told me that a piece of wood had probably become trapped in the sluice gates preventing them from closing completely.", "source": "commons-desc-page"}, "DateTimeOriginal": {"value": "2009-03-31", "source": "commons-desc-page"}, "Credit": {"value": "From <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/1281087\">geograph.org.uk</a>", "source": "commons-desc-page"}, "Artist": {"value": "<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.geograph.org.uk/profile/560\">David Hawgood</a>", "source": "commons-desc-page"}, "Attribution": {"value": "David Hawgood", "source": "commons-desc-page", "hidden": ""}, "LicenseShortName": {"value": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "source": "commons-desc-page", "hidden": ""}, "UsageTerms": {"value": "Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alik